riscv64 zkVM: JitInlineAdditionalMultiplier is release-settable and applied in release

The inliner's profitability model weighs code growth against an estimated call-site
benefit; on the zkVM code size is free and every avoided call saves the executed call
sequence, prolog/epilog and argument moves (about a fifth of all steps in the traced
Nethermind block are call ABI overhead). The Checked-JIT inlining dump of the guest shows
"call site: unprofitable inline" as the dominant failure (12.9k sites), including hot
small callees in the trie and RLP code.

JitInlineAdditionalMultiplier already existed as a DEBUG-only knob added to the
multiplier of both the default and the extended policy. It is now RELEASE_CONFIG and the
addition happens in release builds too; the default stays 0 (no behaviour change). bflat
passes it for the zisk libc.

Measured on nine mainnet blocks with ziskemu, same JIT with the knob at 0 vs 3:
-2.2% executed steps (-35.3M), identical outputs; guest code grew 3%.

